---
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import './OpenSource.css';
---

<section id="open-source" class="section oss">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="section-kicker">Licensing</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Genuinely open source</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">
      The commercial RMMs are excellent and expensive. The open-source ones are
      either monitoring-only or carry licenses that stop you building on them.
      Everwas picks a lane and stays in it.
    </p>

    <div class="oss-grid">
      <div class="oss-card card">
        <div class="oss-badge">AGPL-3.0</div>
        <h3><Icon name="lucide:server-cog" size={18} /> Server</h3>
        <p>
          Run it, modify it, offer it as a service. If you improve it and host
          it for others, those improvements come back. That's the deal, and
          it's a good one.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="oss-card card">
        <div class="oss-badge oss-badge-cyan">Apache-2.0</div>
        <h3><Icon name="lucide:box" size={18} /> Agent</h3>
        <p>
          Permissive on purpose: the binary that runs on customer machines
          should be auditable, embeddable, and redistributable without a
          lawyer in the loop.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="oss-card card">
        <div class="oss-badge oss-badge-dim">DCO</div>
        <h3><Icon name="lucide:git-pull-request" size={18} /> Contributions</h3>
        <p>
          Sign-off, not CLA. Your contribution stays yours, the project stays
          open, and nobody can relicense the commons out from under you.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="oss-more">
      Two licenses is a deliberate design, not indecision.
      <a href="/licensing">Read the full reasoning <Icon name="lucide:arrow-right" size={14} /></a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
